<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gaudavaho</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Gaudavadha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gaudavaho, 'the slaying of the Gauda king', is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a> poem written in the 8th century by Vakpatiraja, court poet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yashovarman-of-kannauj">Yashovarman of Kannauj</a>. It celebrates Yashovarman's campaigns, including his victory over a king of Gauda and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>, though the slaying itself is only briefly described. The poem is an important source for north Indian history after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a> and for Prakrit literature.</p></div>
